{
  "$comment": "The day/night cycle (docs/DAY_NIGHT.md, ADR 0034): the world clock, the sun's path, how the sun, moon, stars and sky are drawn, and the sky keyframes over the day. Colours are sRGB #rrggbb. Run `node shared/look/daynight.mjs` after editing; CI fails if crates/dwell-look/src/daynight.gen.rs is stale. The clock is public API: every build on a compatibility line must agree on it (tree growth counts its days).",
  "clock": {
    "$comment": "Seconds of daylight and of night on face A; startHour is the 24-hour clock at world tick 0 (a new world starts in the morning).",
    "dayS": 1200,
    "nightS": 600,
    "startHour": 7
  },
  "sunPath": {
    "$comment": "The sun rises toward riseYawDeg (yaw 0 = +Z, 90 = +X), culminates noonElevationDeg above face A's horizon a quarter turn to the right of it, and sets opposite. The moon is always opposite the sun (BIFACIAL_WORLD.md \u00a76).",
    "riseYawDeg": 31,
    "noonElevationDeg": 60
  },
  "sun": {
    "$comment": "The disc's angular radius, how much larger it looks on the horizon, its limb darkening (0 none, 1 dark edge), and its halo: a tight bloom and a broad glow (Gaussian widths in degrees, strengths 0\u20131, scaled by each keyframe's glow).",
    "radiusDeg": 1.5,
    "horizonScale": 1.4,
    "limbDarkening": 0.35,
    "bloomDeg": 2.5,
    "bloom": 0.2,
    "glowDeg": 28,
    "glow": 0.3
  },
  "moon": {
    "$comment": "The disc, its phases (a cycle of cycleDays in-game days; startPhase at day 0: 0 new, 0.5 full), its surface (maria darkness, earthshine on the unlit part) and its cool halo (a bloom and a glow, as the sun's, scaled by each keyframe's moonGlow and the lit share). newMoonLight is the share of the moonlight left at new moon, so every night stays readable.",
    "radiusDeg": 1.2,
    "cycleDays": 8,
    "startPhase": 0.5,
    "color": "#d6dbe6",
    "maria": 0.45,
    "earthshine": 0.06,
    "bloomDeg": 2.5,
    "bloom": 0.45,
    "glowDeg": 12,
    "glow": 0.14,
    "glowColor": "#c6d6ff",
    "newMoonLight": 0.55
  },
  "stars": {
    "$comment": "Procedural stars on the turning sky: cells per radian, the share of cells with a star, size in pixels, peak brightness, twinkle depth (0\u20131) and rate, and how far up from the horizon (elevation sine) they fade in.",
    "cells": 220,
    "density": 0.06,
    "sizePx": 1.3,
    "brightness": 0.8,
    "twinkle": 0.35,
    "twinkleHz": 0.9,
    "horizonFade": 0.18
  },
  "sky": {
    "$comment": "The gradient from horizon to zenith (colour = mix(horizon, zenith, elevation^gradientPower)); the warm band along the horizon toward the sun (its height as an elevation sine, and how tightly it gathers toward the sun's azimuth). Below the horizon, the sky behind the world (past the rim, down a portal) is the viewer's horizon haze near the horizon, a ray there crossing the viewer's face's air to the rim, giving way to the other face's sky from behindFrom to behindTo (elevation sines below the horizon: ~1° to ~27°), so there is no hard edge where the two faces' skies meet (docs/BUGS.md 12).",
    "gradientPower": 0.5,
    "bandHeight": 0.3,
    "bandSpread": 3,
    "behindFrom": 0.02,
    "behindTo": 0.45
  },
